{title: The Solid Rock (My Hope Is Built)}
{artist: Edward Mote / William B. Bradbury}
{genre: Hymn, Gospel}
{style: Traditional}
{flow: Response}
{key: F}
{tempo: 110}
{time: 3/4}
{c: Chords checked against the four-part harmony of the Cyber Hymnal score (The Hymnbook (Richmond, Virginia: Presbyterian Church in the United States, 1955), # 368)}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[C]My [F]hope is built on [C]nothing less
[F]Than [Bb]Jesus’ blood and [C]righteous[F]ness.
[F]I dare not trust the [C]sweetest frame,
[F]But [Bb]wholly trust in [C]Jesus’ [F]name.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
[F]On Christ the solid [Bb]rock I stand,
[Bb]All [F]other ground is sinking [C]sand;
[C]All [F]other [Bb]ground [F]is [C]sinking [F]sand.
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
[C]When [F]darkness seems to [C]hide His face,
[F]I [Bb]rest on His un[C]changing [F]grace.
[F]In every high and [C]stormy gale,
[F]My [Bb]anchor holds with[C]in the [F]veil.
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
[C]His [F]oath, His cove[C]nant, His blood,
[F]Sup[Bb]port me in the [C]whelming [F]flood.
[F]When all around my [C]soul gives way,
[F]He [Bb]then is all my [C]Hope and [F]Stay.
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
[C]When [F]He shall come with [C]trumpet sound,
[F]Oh [Bb]may I then in [C]Him be [F]found.
[F]Dressed in His righteous[C]ness alone,
[F]Fault[Bb]less to stand be[C]fore the [F]throne.
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}
